#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163117} :activity/id #uuid "a75a57eb-1a2d-59cc-9314-4e4f1453c437" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048327 :topic/id #uuid "9576099a-1e2b-5b2b-bca5-c78638a7b4b8" :topic/math-academy-id 3137} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163344 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a company is about to elect a new union representative. We're interested in determining the proportion $p$ of a company's employees that will vote for \"candidate $A.$\"\n\nIf the company has $N = 50$ employees, and $30$ of them will vote for candidate $A,$ then the proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{30}{50} = 0.6.\n$$\n\nIn other words, $60\\%$ of the company's employees will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $N$ is the **population size**\n\n- $p$ is the **population proportion**\n\n- The number of employees that will vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nNp = 50\\cdot 0.6 = 30.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, our goal is to build foundations for estimating $p$ in cases where the population size $N$ is very large. Note that although the population size $N$ is not very large in this example, we can still use it to help develop the key ideas.\n\nWe start by modeling the response from each member of the population as a random variable $X_i,$ defined by\n\n![](images/76/76b2ae3dd8206bfdbe208d0e037bd1efa5614101917cf6f981df078cb3cdc8cf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50.$ \n\nCalculating the probability that a randomly selected member of the population will vote for candidate $A$ is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{i}=1) & =\\frac{\\text{the number of members that will vote for candidate }A}{\\text{population size}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Np}{N} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{50} \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to note that the $X_i$'s are *dependent!* For example,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1) \\neq P(X_2 = 1 ).\n$$\n\nTo see why, note that $P(X_2 = 1) = 0.6,$ as before. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1) & =\\frac{(\\text{the number of members that will vote for candidate }A)-1}{\\text{population size}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{Np-1}{N-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{50\\cdot 0.6-1}{50-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-1}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{49} \\\\  & ≈0.592 \\\\  & \\ne P({X}_{2}=1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's interpret this result. We know that the first member will vote for candidate $A.$ So, to compute the conditional probability that the second member will *also* vote for candidate $A,$ we consider a reduced population with $N-1 = 49$ members, of which only $30-1 = 29$ will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nCalculating $P(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 0)$ works similarly, as we'll see in the following example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3fb9dab1-fabb-573d-8f7b-d06c37474163" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6461 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b7e31dee-05b0-5626-92d4-2ad066905c81" :step/math-academy-id 39024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161703 :question/id #uuid "1ad81fed-5e71-5df6-abc4-a09cd5c432fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555845}] :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "3959ad96-797b-5cb6-937c-25ee4bd8fbf5" :step/math-academy-id 39025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161704 :question/id #uuid "b162ec77-b73f-50f7-b2d2-b6c82bdb7d67" :question/math-academy-id "e-10363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441123}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"} :step/id #uuid "1a5db991-df51-5d92-b1cc-245ea8960755" :step/math-academy-id 39030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161705 :question/id #uuid "a623014c-203f-5c40-87b5-e847782a8ea1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051082}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555835}] :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "0c4a9d24-fd2f-52ed-94cd-513aa0657bab" :step/math-academy-id 39031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163345 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32bc52b6268f6c5eeb9a6f07fff58c308871cc650541c16a4c595faf81e833df.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,n.$ \n\nIt can be shown that the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables *provided that* the population size $N$ is *significantly larger* than the sample size $n$ (which we denote as $N \\gg n$). In practice, this typically means that the sample size should be no larger than $5\\%$ of the population size.\n\nThe assumption that the population size is much larger than the sample size is true in many cases. For example, the U.S. voting population consists of hundreds of millions of people, yet a typical sample from this population might consist of only a few thousand voters. \n\nLet's now consider the following statistic:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i\n$$\n\nwhere $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).$ \n\nSince the $X_i$'s take the value $1$ if the $i$th element has the characteristic or $0$ if the $i$th element doesn't have the characteristic, the statistic $X$ can be used to count the number of elements with the characteristic. In other words, $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic.\n\nWe wish to determine the sampling distribution of $X.$ Note the following:\n\n- Each $X_i$ can be modeled as a Bernoulli random variable, i.e., $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).$\n\n- Since we're assuming that $N\\gg n,$ it follows that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are (approximately) *independent and identically distributed* (I.I.D) Bernoulli random variables.\n\n- It can be shown that a sum of $n$ I.I.D. Bernoulli random variables with parameter $p$ is a binomial random variable $B(n,p)$ (we'll prove this in a separate lesson). Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p).\n$$\n\nWe summarize this process using the following flow chart.\n\n![](images/f5/f50d54432c86d4b41ed842218a70b93617457de3e518aa88cbe6ea28762f37ea.png)\n\nFinally, using the formulas for the mean and variance of a Binomial random variable, we have the following approximations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "77f66879-2dda-57f9-b96c-2ad1166282c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6477 :tutorial/title "The Number of Sample Elements With a Characteristic"} :step/id #uuid "7f0864d1-c890-56d0-ad56-9af735a0fb71" :step/math-academy-id 39212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163346 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a population of size $N=1\\,000$ of which the proportion $p=0.5$ has a particular characteristic. We denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a801fdb0d37e38011713448593b185b440445d6a57b0ff42e00c21e66401f6bf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,1\\,000.$\n\nTechnically speaking, as we saw previously, the $X_i$'s are *dependent*.\n\nThis time, however, since the population size $N = 1\\,000$ is large, we may *approximate* that the $X_i$'s are *independent*. \n\nTo see why, we first note that \n\n$$\nP(X_2=1) = \\dfrac{Np}{N} = 0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $P(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1) & =\\frac{Np-1}{N-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000\\cdot 0.5-1}{1\\,000-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{500-1}{999} \\\\  & =\\frac{499}{999} \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we've shown that\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1) \\approx P(X_2 = 1),\n$$\n\nsuggesting that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are (approximately) independent." :tutorial/id #uuid "a129ef6b-606a-5b23-8f65-0573d4422fcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6479 :tutorial/title "Independence in Large Populations"} :step/id #uuid "16f70a0f-60a9-55ce-a435-a9dd8530d4d9" :step/math-academy-id 39214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163347 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/46/4624ca7ee7660803ddc7165252c3725030b13ab255f56dc5d87e23090c2363bf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,n.$ \n\nWe know that if the population sample size $n$ is small compared to the population size $N,$ then the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic. Therefore, we can form an *estimate* for the population proportion $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n} = \\dfrac1 n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i =  \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, for the mean of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot np \\\\  & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot np(1-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is, it has mean $p$ and variance $\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8ecb0abb-cc8a-58b3-b310-fabeeb9c4aca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6480 :tutorial/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "656c4a52-6bb0-59f8-878d-58db462b17ee" :step/math-academy-id 39215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163128}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163349 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of $\\widehat{\\,p}$  is called the **standard error** of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}}\n$$\n\nIf $p$ is unknown, we can replace it with its estimate $\\widehat{\\,p}$ in the standard error formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}]  = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nNote that we have written $\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$ to denote that the above expression is an estimate of $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}] .$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34af16d9-40e0-5de6-9e2d-753be4cea679" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6484 :tutorial/title "The Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "8e2f6423-528f-586d-8112-c73e1f771a8a" :step/math-academy-id 39234 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163118}}] :activity/title "Sampling Proportions From Finite Populations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162231}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486511} :activity/id #uuid "ce9af402-f0b5-551b-a4ad-0194ff218092" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048327 :topic/id #uuid "9576099a-1e2b-5b2b-bca5-c78638a7b4b8" :topic/math-academy-id 3137} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019c2c01-30dd-5c5f-8b90-73f9344dc881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.25,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485766} {:answer/id #uuid "02fb1520-a9c6-5a73-a112-44948c9ae426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.25,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485769} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc72d5e-57f2-5ca3-8e38-bf9dd994405d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.75,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485819} {:answer/id #uuid "37521a95-ddd5-5ea7-b2b3-b4bdd56c6670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.25,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485870} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a5fb5b-749a-576d-91ab-0632592d5e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.75,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486092}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485766} :answer-field/id #uuid "af58c111-f99a-57f4-a10e-1136008a2d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5101e52-f007-51fe-8d87-cde53f7bb6a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-146444" :question/problem "Suppose that $25%$ of a high school student population like mathematics. A random sample of $n=25$ high school students is taken from the population and asked about their favorite subject.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of students in the sample who like mathematics? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=25%=0.25,$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =25%=0.25, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{25} \\\\  & =0.0075.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "223efbcd-a7aa-5d4e-9f1a-1c7b82ee73e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486478}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519d160c-0ea4-58a9-a947-73fc7f0938a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.05,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485913} {:answer/id #uuid "8a689ad1-9962-554c-8093-e6837ab2abcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.95,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486011} {:answer/id #uuid "c24a5121-16cd-59c2-9c8d-82042ff6cfe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.95,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486113} {:answer/id #uuid "d000e6d6-4f1c-5d12-881f-a9853df1ad21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.05,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0049" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486139} {:answer/id #uuid "d10e4dfb-9f2d-5ff6-9adf-833a9cdd919b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.05,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485913} :answer-field/id #uuid "5432d2f4-4b49-5e55-a1cc-c6c715583c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fff98227-c5ba-54d3-b84c-be9e152eefa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-146451" :question/problem "It is known that  $5%$ of the researchers in a particular university work in neurobiology. A random sample of $n=15$ researchers is taken from the population and asked about their work area.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of researchers in the sample working in neurobiology? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=5%=0.05,$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =5%=0.05, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.05(1-0.05)}{15} \\\\  & ≈0.0032\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "4b70bf6a-cea2-5190-923a-cd1b5791a62b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe3c289-0f8e-54d7-ae79-90ed91af84a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=84,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485856} {:answer/id #uuid "6b18e2b2-d24f-565d-bc92-47ebd538d184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=81,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485948} {:answer/id #uuid "80d05a6e-4274-5fc6-8882-70b335cc878f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=84,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=36.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485992} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a24b85-d6ca-5625-8bec-1f5d00428da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=84,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=33.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486123} {:answer/id #uuid "e64e2775-6379-524b-b45e-66d7d130796f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=81,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=33.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486123} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d4f1e19-3dfb-5e8c-a60d-6c3ee527f11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6975e0db-6a89-53e9-8d9a-8975ae20b85d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146403" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=140$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,140.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{140}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60%=0.6,\\,n=140.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(140,0.6)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =84 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.6\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =33.6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "74a5ebf6-1c92-5485-bb12-c261b4d4f72a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486499}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ec1015-67ae-532c-90ae-ecdaface5f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.4,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485960} {:answer/id #uuid "a2dbf744-dd49-5c6d-a61b-984a7259504c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486057} {:answer/id #uuid "adf531a6-7319-5b80-9274-0509621d73e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486077} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4631fc-533d-5b33-96dd-c7fb0f7aac14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.4,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486133} {:answer/id #uuid "fd23b3bb-07c4-5f49-97bc-5ab8e4e8d978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486227} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c7b60b-ebc0-552f-bb22-591e7a44c23f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1024622-b9b9-5914-883d-61f4135d4db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-146442" :question/problem "Suppose that $60%$ of the child population enrolled in a baseball academy is right-handed. A random sample of $n=20$ children is taken from the population and asked about their dominant hand.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of right-handed children in the sample? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=60%=0.6,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =60%=0.6, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{20} \\\\  & =0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "97e146b0-570c-5e63-97c5-a9b64f3bdfb7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486471}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6e9aa3-7579-5a88-aab9-009a45405b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.42)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485844} {:answer/id #uuid "4d29a851-eb8b-501c-abce-b34ca4a45796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B(66,0.42)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485907} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfb1579-730f-5017-931e-2e7002367e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B(28,0.42)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486015} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bcdc0f-1389-5e8c-a2f7-0ffa32acc877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B(66,0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486083} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ae63a2-5d82-5b0e-b38b-f7334456bc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485907} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b6f92b-32ff-54a7-9dff-a149716ae98e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bfc489e6-3fde-599d-8b94-fa54c6ab85f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-146400" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=66$ from a population where $42%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,66.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{66}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=42%=0.42,\\,n=66.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(66,0.42).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d491de4f-dae6-58b0-899a-0ecd2bc31627" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486488}}] :activity/title "Sampling Proportions From Finite Populations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485760}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555915} :activity/id #uuid "566bafbe-17d8-59ff-ae1a-7f0098a7748d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604